Debug.Assert(RET &lt;= _items.Length);
</code>
Justification: The maximum capacity of the list is dictated by the length of the internal array `_items`. Therefore, the return value (`RET`) should be less than or equal to this length.
--------------------
Debug.Assert(OLD(_items.Length) <= ArrayList.MaxArrayLength);
</code>
Please note that in C#, `ArrayList._items.Length` is equivalent to the size or count of the array which is the number of elements actually stored in the array. Thus, the current count of the array is `ArrayList._items.Length`, which indicates the total capacity of the array. The assertion checks if the size doesn't exceed the maximum array length. 

However, please note that this assertion might be unnecessary or redundant, because C# lists and arrays have a size limit determined by the maximum array length, which in most cases is exceeded only in very specific circumstances, such as creating an array with very large numbers, and not when adding elements to an array that has reached its maximum size or limit.
--------------------
// @@@ If the list contains item, last index of item must be less than array size
Debug.Assert(!item || LastIndexOf(item) < _items.Length);
